    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Bank Statement Loans for Freelancers: How Your Deposits Can Qualify You When Your Tax Returns Can't</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've been freelancing for at least a year, your deposits are probably a more accurate reflection of what you earn than your tax returns are. Every legitimate deduction you've taken, every business expense your accountant wrote off, every depreciation entry on your Schedule C, has reduced the qualifying income a conventional lender will use. A bank statement loan is built specifically for that mismatch. It qualifies you on what actually came in, not what was left after the tax strategy did its work.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What a Bank Statement Loan Actually Does</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Instead of using your tax return net income to calculate what you qualify for, a bank statement loan uses your actual deposits over the prior 12 to 24 months. The lender applies an expense ratio to your total deposits to estimate your net qualifying income, then uses that number for debt-to-income calculations. For freelancers whose gross income far exceeds their reported net income, that shift in how qualifying income is calculated can change the entire qualification picture.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The expense ratio is the key variable. Most lenders apply a standard ratio, often 50%, meaning half your total deposits are counted as qualifying income. If your actual business overhead runs lower than that, some lenders will accept documentation of a lower expense ratio, which increases your qualifying income further. A graphic designer whose primary expenses are software subscriptions and a home office has very different actual overhead than a contractor who buys materials and equipment. If your real expense ratio is closer to 30%, a lender who recognizes that will qualify you for significantly more.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Creatives Benefit From This Product Specifically</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's something specific to how many creatives and freelancers earn that makes bank statement loans particularly well suited to this audience. Freelance creative income is often lumpy. A photographer might have a slow January and a very strong March when three weddings and a corporate shoot land in the same month. A writer might receive a large advance payment followed by smaller monthly royalty deposits. A designer might close several large projects in the same quarter.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Conventional underwriting treats that volatility as a risk. A bank statement loan treats the total over 12 or 24 months as the relevant number, smoothing out the monthly variation and producing a qualifying income based on what the full period actually generated.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What Bank Statement Loans Cost Compared to Conventional</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Bank statement loans carry higher rates than conventional mortgages, reflecting the reduced documentation and the Non-QM designation. The gap varies by lender, credit score, down payment, and market conditions. For a freelancer who can qualify conventionally at a rate that supports their purchase price, conventional is the better choice. For a freelancer whose conventional qualifying income doesn't get them to the purchase price they need, the rate premium on a bank statement loan is the cost of access, and that tradeoff is often worth making.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Down payment requirements are typically 10 to 20% depending on the lender and the loan amount. Credit score requirements vary but most programs want 660 or above, with better pricing for scores above 700. Reserve requirements apply as well, typically several months of total housing costs held in liquid accounts after closing.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Pull 12 and 24 months of deposits from your business account and calculate your average monthly total.</strong> Apply a 50% expense ratio as a starting estimate. That annualized figure is your approximate bank statement qualifying income.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare that number to your conventional qualifying income from your tax returns.</strong> If the bank statement number is meaningfully higher and gets you to the purchase price you need, a bank statement loan is worth pursuing.</li>
      <li><strong>Document your actual expense ratio if it's below 50%.</strong> Gather receipts, software subscriptions, and any recurring business costs. If your real overhead is lower than the standard ratio, some lenders will adjust downward with supporting documentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Make sure your deposit history is clean and consolidated.</strong> Fragmented deposits across multiple accounts, irregular large transfers, or non-business income mixed in will complicate underwriting. The cleaner the account history, the smoother the process.</li>
      <li><strong>Shop at least two Non-QM lenders before you commit.</strong> Bank statement loan rates and expense ratio policies vary more across lenders than conventional rates do. A second quote costs nothing and can meaningfully affect both your rate and your qualifying income calculation.</li>
    </ol>

